  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Trending seasonably cooler this weekend into \nnext week with bulk of precipitation confined near and southeast\nof Lake Erie\n\nFollowing one of the warmest starts to November on record in\ncentral PA, (see climate section) temperatures will trend \nseasonably cooler/lower this weekend into next week. Persistent\ncyclonic flow over the next 5 days or so with embedded \nshortwave disturbances will offer ample opportunities for precip\ndownwind or southeast of Lake Erie while limiting moisture in \nthe lee of (to the east) of the Allegheny Mountains. A more \nsubstantial long wave trough may attempt to carve out over the \neastern U.S. by the end of the week. \n\nIn the shorter term, secondary cold front will cross US-219\nlater this afternoon accompanied by a narrow band of rain\nshowers. A compact and seasonably strong closed upper low \npivots to the southeast from the thumb of lower MI through CPA\ntonight into tomorrow before lifting out of the area by Sunday \nmorning. This feature accompanied by cool air aloft and mean \nwest to NW flow will bring scattered to numerous showers to \nstart the first full weekend of November. Extensive cloud cover \nand a WNW wind should provide a floor for min temps. A few wet \nsnowflakes can't be ruled out at the peak elevations of the NW \nAllegheny Plateau and Laurel Mtn summits, but no accumulation is\nexpected. Saturday will be noticeably cooler and feel rather \nchilly given how warm it has been during the day over the past \nweek or so.",
